INT. NORTHERN CHECKPOINT - INSPECTION BOOTH - NIGHT

A booth of steel and glass. A scanner hums. Beyond the window, a line of vehicles stretches into fog.

OFFICER OKAFOR, 40s, sits behind the desk. Opposite her, a figure stands in a long coat, face hidden by a respirator mask.

OKAFOR
Papers.

THE TRAVELER
(sliding a card across)
They are all there.

OKAFOR
(reading, a long beat)
This card was issued in a year that has not happened yet.

THE TRAVELER
(beat)
Then it is early. I apologise.

OKAFOR
(setting it down)
Remove the mask.

THE TRAVELER
(beat)
I would prefer not to.

OKAFOR
That was not a request.

SUPERVISOR DUNNE, 50s, enters from the back office, jacket over one arm.

DUNNE
(low, to Okafor)
Problem?

OKAFOR
(not turning)
The scanner says there is nobody inside the coat.

DUNNE
(glancing at the screen)
The scanner is old.

OKAFOR
(beat)
The scanner is new.

THE TRAVELER
(slowly, lifting the mask with both hands)
Please. Do not be frightened.

OKAFOR
(rising, hand on her holster)
Step back.

DUNNE
(raising a hand)
Officer. Easy.

The mask comes away. Underneath is a woman of perhaps thirty, tired, ordinary, with a scar through one eyebrow.

THE TRAVELER
(quietly)
My name is Mirela. I was born in this booth.

OKAFOR
(a laugh, no humour)
That is not possible.

MIRELA
(beat)
My mother stood where you are standing. She was a guard, and she told me, one day a woman will come with a card from tomorrow.

DUNNE
(carefully)
And what did she say to do?

MIRELA
(beat)
She said to let me through.

OKAFOR
(to Dunne)
She is lying.

DUNNE
(to Mirela)
Who is your mother?

MIRELA
(simply)
Your wife.

OKAFOR
(stunned)
Sir?

DUNNE
(very still)
My wife has no children.

LUKAS (V.O.)
(over the intercom, crackling)
Booth three, we have a breach on the east lane. Repeat, east lane.

OKAFOR
(into the radio)
Hold position, Lukas.

DUNNE
(at the same time)
Send everyone to the east gate.

OKAFOR ^
(overlapping, sharp)
Sir, you cannot order that.

DUNNE
(softly, to Mirela)
Tell me what she looked like.

MIRELA
(beat, gentle)
Like you, but she laughed.

DUNNE
(a long silence, then hoarse)
Open the barrier.

OKAFOR
(beat)
Sir.

DUNNE
(turning on her, gently)
Open it.

OKAFOR
(after a long moment, pressing a button)
Barrier is open.

MIRELA
(lifting the mask, but not wearing it)
Thank you. Both of you.

DUNNE
(beat)
Will I see her?

MIRELA
(at the door, a small smile)
You already did. That is why it is a loop.

She exits. Okafor and Dunne stand side by side, watching the barrier rise into the fog.
